Reject non-identical character conversions as well as iconv errors.

Solaris iconv can substitute a character and return a positive count instead
of failing with EILSEQ. Treat that as an out-of-range character so that
strict (#X) expansion fails and non-strict (#) retains its byte fallback.
Commit the converted bytes only after an identical conversion, so failed
conversions do not prevent the byte fallback. This preserves the existing
upstream D04parameter.ztst expectations.

The upstream report describes the same behavior on BSD systems:
https://www.zsh.org/mla/workers/2023/msg00869.html (workers/52140).
Upstream implements this behavior in:
https://github.com/zsh-users/zsh/commit/02e33c54d85208c7d9b96d91a26d04069ff19ed2
Author: Jun-ichi Takimoto <takimoto-j@kba.biglobe.ne.jp>
Date: Wed Sep 27 01:56:47 2023 +0900
Subject: 52169: a few more improvements of (#) flag

This patch is a minimal adaptation of the upstream error handling to 5.9.2.
The upstream commit also refactors character conversion into ucs4tomb();
that broader refactoring is not included here. Both implementations reject
any nonzero iconv return value and leave the caller's output position
unchanged on failure. Linux reports an unrepresentable character with
(size_t)-1; Solaris may instead report a positive substitution count.

--- a/Src/utils.c
+++ b/Src/utils.c
@@ -7022,6 +7022,7 @@
 		} else {
 #   ifdef HAVE_ICONV
 		    ICONV_CONST char *inptr = inbuf;
+		    char *outptr = t;
 		    const char *codesetstr = nl_langinfo(CODESET);
     	    	    inbytes = 4;
 		    outbytes = 6;
@@ -7065,12 +7066,13 @@
 			zerr("cannot do charset conversion (iconv failed)");
 			CHARSET_FAILED();
 		    }
-                    count = iconv(cd, &inptr, &inbytes, &t, &outbytes);
+                    count = iconv(cd, &inptr, &inbytes, &outptr, &outbytes);
 		    iconv_close(cd);
-		    if (count == (size_t)-1) {
+		    if (count != 0) {
                         zerr("character not in range");
 			CHARSET_FAILED();
 		    }
+		    t = outptr;
 		    if ((how & GETKEY_UPDATE_OFFSET) && s - sstart < *misc)
 			(*misc) += count;
 #   else
